WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.493
Sara was fond of the Large family because they looked happy.

2
00:00:05.494 --> 00:00:10.321
But she liked the gentleman next door because he looked un

3
00:00:10.322 --> 00:00:14.233
happy. The school's servants were always talking about

4
00:00:14.234 --> 00:00:19.640
him. Sara learned that the English gentleman had become ill after losing his

5
00:00:19.641 --> 00:00:23.818
fortune. He'd gotten his fortune back, but he was still

6
00:00:23.819 --> 00:00:28.701
sick. "His problems were connected with diamond mines," said the

7
00:00:28.702 --> 00:00:34.959
cook. She glanced at Sara. "We all know they're

8
00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:39.596
trouble." "That man was just like Papa," Sara said to herself.

9
00:00:39.597 --> 00:00:44.729
"He felt just what my Papa felt, only he did not die."

10
00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:50.678
So Sara grew even fonder of the sick gentleman next door.

11
00:00:50.679 --> 00:00:55.067
Whenever she was sent out at night, she stopped outside his house.

12
00:00:55.068 --> 00:01:01.665
She liked to peek into the house and see him sitting in his armchair, his forehead resting in his hand.

13
00:01:01.666 --> 00:01:04.534
She often wished him good night.

14
00:01:04.535 --> 00:01:10.519
"Perhaps you can feel my good wishes even if you can't hear them," she said.

15
00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:17.320
"I hope you'll get well and be happy again. I'm so sorry for you."

16
00:01:17.321 --> 00:01:22.412
She went on her way, feeling comforted and a bit warmer herself.

17
00:01:22.413 --> 00:01:25.533
But she kept worrying about the man.

18
00:01:25.534 --> 00:01:30.659
He looked to Sara like someone who had a lot of trouble on his mind.

19
00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:36.906
Meanwhile Mr. Large and his daughters Janet and Nora often visited the gentleman.

20
00:01:36.907 --> 00:01:43.735
The sick man's name was Mr. Carrisford. Mr. Large's real name was Mr. Carmichael.

21
00:01:43.736 --> 00:01:46.671
He was Mr. Carrisford's lawyer. One

22
00:01:46.672 --> 00:01:52.966
evening Janet told Mr. Carrisford about the Little Girl Who Is Not a Beggar. He

23
00:01:52.967 --> 00:01:56.325
was very interested. Then

24
00:01:56.326 --> 00:02:00.501
Ram Dass told him the story of the monkey's escape. Ram

25
00:02:00.502 --> 00:02:07.010
Dass described the servant girl's cold attic room and her hard, narrow bed. The

26
00:02:07.011 --> 00:02:10.310
story upset Mr. Carrisford. He

27
00:02:10.311 --> 00:02:15.867
was wealthy and comfortable while children were cold and hungry. "I

28
00:02:15.868 --> 00:02:22.233
wonder how many of the attics in London are like that one," he said to Mr. Carmichael. "How

29
00:02:22.234 --> 00:02:28.596
many young servant girls sleep on hard beds while I sleep on soft pillows?" "My

30
00:02:28.597 --> 00:02:33.485
dear fellow," Mr. Carmichael answered in a cheery voice. "You

31
00:02:33.486 --> 00:02:36.335
must stop thinking thoughts like this. Even

32
00:02:36.336 --> 00:02:41.310
if you had all the wealth in the world, you could not improve all the attics in London. You

33
00:02:41.311 --> 00:02:45.076
must focus your thoughts on becoming well." Mr.

34
00:02:45.077 --> 00:02:50.402
Carrisford sat and bit his nails as he stared into the fire. "Do

35
00:02:50.403 --> 00:02:56.372
you think the girl I'm looking for is living in such a horrid place?" he asked suddenly. Mr. Carmichael

36
00:02:56.373 --> 00:02:59.660
looked at him uneasily. He knew

37
00:02:59.661 --> 00:03:05.064
that this kind of thinking was very dangerous to the frail gentleman's health. "I don't

38
00:03:05.065 --> 00:03:09.474
think so," he said finally in a soothing tone. "That girl

39
00:03:09.475 --> 00:03:13.455
was adopted by a wealthy Russian couple." "I must

40
00:03:13.456 --> 00:03:17.509
find her!" Mr. Carrisford went on. "If she

41
00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:21.318
is friendless and penniless, it is my fault! She is

42
00:03:21.319 --> 00:03:25.559
the daughter of my dear friend, Captain Crewe." "Are you

43
00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:31.206
sure his daughter was sent to boarding school in France?" Mr. Carmichael asked. "Perhaps we're

44
00:03:31.207 --> 00:03:34.574
searching for the wrong girl." "I'm not

45
00:03:34.575 --> 00:03:39.736
sure of anything." Mr. Carrisford bit his nails again. "Crewe and

46
00:03:39.737 --> 00:03:42.638
I talked mainly about the mines. Her mother

47
00:03:42.639 --> 00:03:47.514
was French, so I assumed she went to a French school." "The French

48
00:03:47.515 --> 00:03:55.056
headmistress pronounced the student's name Carew instead of Crewe ," Mr. Carmichael reminded him. "So

49
00:03:55.057 --> 00:03:59.081
it's possible that we are on the trail of the wrong girl." "We must

50
00:03:59.082 --> 00:04:01.571
find Crewe's daughter!" Mr. Carrisford

51
00:04:01.572 --> 00:04:07.408
clutched Mr. Carmichael's arm. "Help me find her!" "Don't worry,"

52
00:04:07.409 --> 00:04:10.695
said Mr. Carmichael. "I'll go

53
00:04:10.696 --> 00:04:13.073
to Moscow to look for the girl. If she's

54
00:04:13.074 --> 00:04:17.068
not the right girl, we will keep searching." "If I

55
00:04:17.069 --> 00:04:21.805
were able to travel, I would go with you," said Mr. Carrisford. "But I

56
00:04:21.806 --> 00:04:28.850
am too ill. I can only sit here wrapped in fur and staring at the fire. And when

57
00:04:28.851 --> 00:04:32.817
I look into it, I see Crewe's face gazing back at me. And he's

58
00:04:32.818 --> 00:04:38.284
asking me a question: 'Tom, where is my little girl?'" Mr. Carrisford

59
00:04:38.285 --> 00:04:42.684
grabbed Mr. Carmichael's hand desperately. "I must

60
00:04:42.685 --> 00:04:47.065
be able to answer him!" * *

61
00:04:47.066 --> 00:04:53.061
* On the other side of the wall, Sara was sitting in her room, talking to Mel the

62
00:04:53.062 --> 00:04:58.394
rat. "It was very hard to be a princess today, Mel," she

63
00:04:58.395 --> 00:05:03.263
said. "It gets harder as the weather gets colder and the streets get wetter."

64
00:05:03.264 --> 00:05:08.662
Lavinia had laughed at Sara's skirt, spattered with mud from the street.

65
00:05:08.663 --> 00:05:12.854
But Sara had not responded.

66
00:05:12.855 --> 00:05:19.578
"You can't snap at people if you're a princess," she explained to the rat.

67
00:05:19.579 --> 00:05:25.466
"But it was such a cold afternoon. And it's so cold tonight."

68
00:05:25.467 --> 00:05:30.969
Sara put her head in her lap. "Oh, Papa," she whispered.

69
00:05:30.970 --> 00:05:40.642
"It seems like such a long time since I was your little girl!"

